Concretes
description: every way a node is physically stored and written
- >
  A **concrete** records *how and where* a node's value is physically stored. Every materialized
  node carries exactly one, and it is orthogonal to the node's *type* and its *format*: a string
  may live inline in a document, in its own file, or as bytes a format decodes — same value,
  different concrete.
- >
  There are two independent axes. The **storage** axis answers *where, in what language*: a
  portion of a text file, a whole file, opaque bytes, a directory. The **representation** axis
  answers *how it is written*: a string picks a style, a number picks a notation, a container
  picks flow or block. This chapter opens with the general laws — the storage axis, the choosing
  rules, the layout invariants — and then catalogs the languages, concrete by concrete:
- >
  Representations are **not different values** — the spellings decode identically — but the
  representation is worth keeping: it is what makes a string `"~"` distinguishable from a null
  `~` on screen, and what a schema may want to pin ("this id is hex", "this note is a literal
  block"). A non-default spelling is classified from the authored token and preserved end to
  end; the default costs nothing.
